  "Question": "The largest integer \\(n\\) for which \\(n^{200}\\lt5^{300}\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(8\\)",
    "B": "\\(9\\)",
    "C": "\\(10\\)",
    "D": "\\(11\\)",
    "E": "\\(12\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Rewrite both sides as hundredth powers to remove the large exponents:\n\\[\nn^{200}=(n^2)^{100},\\qquad 5^{300}=(5^3)^{100}=125^{100}.\n\\]\nFor nonnegative bases, taking the same positive integer power preserves their order. Since \\(n^2\\ge0\\), the original inequality is therefore equivalent to\n\\[\nn^2<125,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad |n|<\\sqrt{125}.\n\\]\nNow \\(11^2=121<125<144=12^2\\), so \\(11<\\sqrt{125}<12\\). Thus the allowed integers are \\(-11,-10,\\ldots,11\\), and the largest is \\(\\boxed{11}\\), choice D.",
